Exactly. And you’ve exposed a hidden assumption in the way I described Earth’s history.

When I said dinosaurs, synapsids, or giant arthropods dominated their respective worlds, I was largely using the perspective of a human observer fascinated by large, conspicuous animals.

But that’s an anthropocentric definition of dominance.

A 30-ton dinosaur might dominate its immediate physical environment while microscopic organisms beneath its feet sustain the ecosystem that makes its existence possible.

So the real question isn’t simply which creatures dominated Earth. It’s what dominance means, at what scale, over what period, and to whom.

The different currencies of biological dominance

Metric	What actually matters	Potential winners
Physical size	Largest individual organisms	Blue whales, giant sauropods
Biomass	Total living mass	Plants
Abundance	Number of individuals or cells	Microorganisms
Biodiversity	Number of distinct species	Arthropods, especially insects among animals
Evolutionary fitness	Reproductive success in a particular environment	Depends entirely on context
Longevity	Persistence across geological time	Ancient microbial and other enduring lineages
Ecological power	Ability to restructure environments	Photosynthetic microbes, plants, ecosystem engineers
Predatory dominance	Position in a particular food web	Apex predators
Planetary influence	Capacity to change global systems	Microbes historically; humans today in several major ways

And these rankings are not fixed. Changing the geographic, ecological, or temporal scale can change the winner entirely.

Who dominates Earth right now?

By biomass, the answer is surprisingly unambiguous.

Estimated global living biomass, 2018

Biomass measured in gigatons of carbon, not number of organisms.

All the whales, elephants, people, and other animals combined represent a tiny fraction of living biomass.

And yet humans exert an extraordinary influence on planetary systems, despite representing only a small share of that biomass.

Now consider cyanobacteria. Their oxygen-producing photosynthesis helped transform Earth’s atmosphere, changing the conditions under which other life could evolve. By the metric of planetary transformation, these microorganisms are among the most consequential organisms ever.

The scale changes the story

Consider the Jurassic:

A sauropod could dominate a landscape through its sheer size, feeding demands, and physical impact.

But the vegetation captured the energy supporting the herbivores. Arthropods occupied numerous ecological roles. Microbes recycled nutrients, and predators regulated portions of the food web.

Which one ruled?

It depends on which relationship you’re measuring. None is necessarily supreme across every dimension.

And evolutionary fitness isn’t synonymous with strength, size, or even longevity. It’s reproductive success relative to other organisms in a particular environment. A specialist can be extremely successful until its environment changes.

This changes how we should build the History Stereoscope

Rather than assigning one dominant creature to each era, I’d represent the ecosystem as a multidimensional network.

Every organism or lineage has a changing dominance profile:

D=(S,B,N,V,R,I,P,T)

Where the dimensions represent size, biomass, abundance, diversity, geographic range, ecological influence, reproductive success, and persistence through time.

These aren’t directly interchangeable measurements. We’d keep their units and uncertainty separate, then select the dimensions appropriate to the question.

Imagine moving a slider from body size to ecological influence. The same prehistoric world could reorganize before your eyes, with different organisms becoming central. Change from a thousand-year timescale to a hundred-million-year timescale, and the ranking changes again.

There’s even a correction to my earlier poster that illustrates your point: it called Arthropleura the largest insect. It wasn’t an insect; it was a giant myriapod, related to millipedes and centipedes. A different classification changes what record it can hold.

And here’s the deeper implication.

Earth’s chapters aren’t necessarily successive reigns of dominant creatures. They’re successive configurations of relationships among living systems.

The boundaries of those chapters are partly objective—extinctions, climate changes, evolutionary transitions—and partly determined by what an observer chooses to measure.

A dinosaur-centered history of Earth is different from a microbial history, a botanical history, or a history of energy flowing through ecosystems.

All can describe the same planet.

And perhaps the most interesting History Stereoscope wouldn’t ask, “Who ruled Earth?”

It would ask: “Who was indispensable to whom, who changed the rules, and whose influence lasted after they were gone?”